# Secrets shared by EVERY destination. Safe for git: it holds references and commands, never a
# raw credential. Values come from `bin/secret`, which prefers an exported shell value and
# otherwise reads 1Password — so `export FOO=… && kamal deploy` still overrides anything here.
#
# ONLY TRULY-COMMON KEYS BELONG IN THIS FILE, AND THAT IS A CORRECTNESS RULE, NOT TIDINESS.
# Kamal resolves secrets with `::Dotenv.parse`, and Kamal::Secrets::Dotenv::InlineCommandSubstitution
# runs EVERY `$(…)` in a parsed file EAGERLY — not lazily per key fetched. This file is read by
# every destination (Kamal::Secrets#secrets_filenames is [secrets-common, secrets.<dest>]), so a
# destination-specific credential parked here makes `kamal deploy -d share` run an `op read` for a
# credential a share container never holds — an extra vault hit, an extra failure mode, and a
# prompt for something the deploy does not use. Per-destination secrets go in:
#
#   .kamal/secrets        base — chunk backend on Unraid (read ONLY when no -d is passed)
#   .kamal/secrets.s3     -d s3
#   .kamal/secrets.share  -d share
#   .kamal/secrets.ovh    -d ovh
#
# A missing .kamal/secrets.<dest> is not an error — secrets_files does `select { File.exist? }`.
#
# VERIFY BEFORE DEPLOYING: `bin/secrets-preflight [dest]`. Kamal expands these through Ruby
# backticks, which discard exit status, so a failed read becomes an EMPTY secret that deploys
# silently rather than an error. The preflight is the only thing that catches that.

# The Rails master key still comes from the file, not 1Password.
# ⚠️ A GIT WORKTREE DOES NOT HAVE config/master.key — it is gitignored and untracked, so a
# worktree checkout resolves this to EMPTY, the container boots to "ArgumentError: Missing
# secret_key_base", the health check fails, and kamal keeps the old container. `cat` on a missing
# file writes to stderr and yields "", which is exactly the silent-empty case above.
# Fix before deploying from a worktree:  cp <main-checkout>/config/master.key config/master.key
RAILS_MASTER_KEY=$(cat config/master.key)

# ghcr.io token — a classic `ghp_` PAT with write:packages (so the Mac can push) and
# read:packages (so the servers can pull the private image). NOT the `gh` CLI token, which lacks
# the packages scope. Every destination pushes/pulls the same image, so this is genuinely common.
KAMAL_REGISTRY_PASSWORD=$(bin/secret KAMAL_REGISTRY_PASSWORD 'op://tinkerex/ghcr.io/password')
